    slug: "psych-graduate-rpm-or-hr",
    title: "I'm a Psych Graduate - Should I Take the Psychometrician Board or Just Work in HR?",
    metaTitle: "Psych Grad - Psychometrician Board or Straight to HR?",
    excerpt: "BS Psych graduate torn between the RPm board and going straight to HR? The honest decision - what the license actually adds, when skipping it is rational, and the timing play that gets you both.",
    metaDescription: "BS Psych graduate torn between the RPm board and going straight to HR? The honest decision - what the license actually adds, when skipping it is rational, and the timing play that gets you both.",
    content: "**The direct answer: take the board — but the honest version has nuance: HR will hire you without the RPm, so skipping it isn't irrational; it's just expensive later. The license costs you months now and pays three specific dividends HR alone never will: the legally protected assessment scope, RA 1080 government eligibility, and the credential that separates you in every psychology-adjacent promotion for the rest of your career. The smart play for most graduates is both, sequenced.** Here's the full decision.\n\n## What the RPm actually adds (be specific, not vague)\n\n[Under RA 10029](https://lisensyaprep.com/blog/psychometrician-board-exam-guide), the Registered Psychometrician license grants the legal scope to administer and score psychological tests under RPsy supervision — which matters because:\n\n1. **Assessment roles are gated.** Testing positions in clinics, hospitals, HR assessment centers, and government psychological services legally prefer or require the license — the exact roles that pay above generic HR entry\n2. **[RA 1080 eligibility](https://lisensyaprep.com/civil-service/government-jobs-cse-eligibility)** — government psychological-services items (PNP, AFP, DOH, courts) open only to eligibles, and the RPm is yours\n3. **The RPsy runway** — if independent practice ever calls, [the psychologist license requires a master's](https://lisensyaprep.com/blog/how-to-become-a-psychologist-philippines) *plus* the foundation the RPm years build\n4. **The Mental Health Act tailwind** — RA 11036's workplace and school mandates keep expanding demand for licensed assessment\n\n## When skipping it is actually rational\n\nHonesty owed: plenty of successful HR careers run on BS Psych alone. If your genuine trajectory is **recruitment, employee relations, compensation, or HR generalist → HR manager**, the market credentials that matter there are experience and later an HR certification — not the RPm. The license's value concentrates in *assessment-flavored* psychology work; pure people-operations careers can skip it without ceiling damage. Know which career you're actually describing.\n\n## The trap in \"work muna ako, saka na mag-board\"\n\nThe universal pattern across every profession we cover: **the license postponed is usually the license never taken.** Work absorbs the review months, the material goes stale, and [the retaker/late-taker penalty is real and documented](https://lisensyaprep.com/blog/board-exam-retake-limits). The exam is annual — miss a cycle and it's a full year, unlike twice-yearly boards. If you're going to take it, take it while the coursework is fresh.\n\n## The sequenced play (what we'd actually advise)\n\n1. **Fresh grad → board first** if family finances allow the review months: the four subjects (75 average, 60 floor) sit closest to your just-finished coursework right now\n2. **Need income immediately → HR job + board in the same year:** an HR day job plus [a structured working-reviewee plan](https://lisensyaprep.com/blog/how-to-study-prc-board-exam-while-working-full-time) for the annual exam — the combination most working RPms actually did\n3. **Either way, decide the RPsy question by year 3:** if practice calls, the master's starts; if HR leadership calls, an HR certification does. The RPm keeps both branches alive; skipping it prunes one\n\n## The Honest Bottom Line\n\nThe RPm is cheap insurance on a career whose direction you can't fully predict at 21. HR will take you without it — and five years from now, the assessment-center lead role, the government item, and the RPsy dream will all ask the same question: *\"Registered ka ba?\"* Months now versus that question forever. (The exam guide · [free reviewers](https://lisensyaprep.com/).)\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**Can I work in HR without the psychometrician license?**\nYes — recruitment, employee relations, and generalist HR roles hire BS Psych graduates without it; the license gates assessment-specific roles.\n\n**Is the psychometrician board exam hard?**\nFour subjects (Theories, Abnormal, I/O, Assessment) at 75% average with a 60% subject floor — demanding but sits closest to fresh coursework, which is why taking it early matters.\n\n**Does the RPm increase salary?**\nIt gates the assessment-flavored roles that pay above generic HR entry and adds RA 1080 government eligibility — the premium is in access more than in an automatic raise.",
    faqSchema: "{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"FAQPage\",\"mainEntity\":[{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Should psychology graduates take the psychometrician board exam or work in HR first?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"For most graduates, take the board — while coursework is fresh, since the exam is annual — because the RPm license gates assessment roles, confers RA 1080 government eligibility, and keeps the psychologist pathway alive, while pure HR careers remain open either way.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Can psychology graduates work in HR without a license?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Yes. Recruitment, employee relations, and HR generalist roles hire BS Psychology graduates without the RPm; the license specifically gates psychological testing and assessment roles and government psychological-services positions.\"}}]}",
